WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.206 --> 00:00:03.310
What's the topic of today's video?

00:00:03.611 --> 00:00:05.206
No, that's far too general.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.200
No, it's not that either.

00:00:08.246 --> 00:00:10.560
No, I already made a video on that one.

00:00:11.884 --> 00:00:13.169
Yes, that's it.

00:00:14.574 --> 00:00:18.058
Hello, everyone. Welcome to the
Langfocus channel, and my name is Paul.

00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:20.898
Today, I'm going to talk
about the Russian language.

00:00:20.923 --> 00:00:22.494
Why is Russian important?

00:00:22.666 --> 00:00:25.723
Well, Russian is one of the
world's major languages.

00:00:25.748 --> 00:00:28.754
It is one of the six official
languages of the United Nations,

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:31.782
and it is the eighth most widely
spoken language in the world

00:00:31.807 --> 00:00:34.559
with around 155
million native speakers

00:00:34.584 --> 00:00:37.185
and around 260
million speakers in total,

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.099
if we include second language speakers.

00:00:39.124 --> 00:00:41.310
It's an official language
in Russia, of course,

00:00:41.335 --> 00:00:45.365
but also in Belarus, Tajikistan,
Kyrgyzstan and Kazakhstan.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.191
It is also an official language

00:00:47.216 --> 00:00:51.072
in 38 other unrecognized states, 
autonomous territories, and regions

00:00:51.097 --> 00:00:53.324
in Ukraine, Moldova and Georgia,

00:00:53.349 --> 00:00:55.480
and also a few localities in Romania,

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.403
and the Russian Economic Zone
in Spitsbergen in Norway.

00:00:58.721 --> 00:01:02.003
It is also widely spoken as a second
language and a lingua franca

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:05.663
throughout much of Central Asia, the
Caucasus region and in Eastern Europe.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.644
Even in a lot of places where
it is not an official language.

00:01:08.669 --> 00:01:10.470
That's why Russian is important.

00:01:10.555 --> 00:01:14.077
Russian is the most widely spoken
language of the Slavic language family.

00:01:14.102 --> 00:01:17.094
Have a look at my video on the
Slavic language family, here,

00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:18.674
to learn more about it in detail.

00:01:18.699 --> 00:01:19.705
But, in brief,

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:23.140
the Slavic languages are a branch of
the Indo-European language family,

00:01:23.165 --> 00:01:26.124
and the beginning of the Slavic
language branch traces back

00:01:26.149 --> 00:01:29.730
to a language called Proto-Slavic
which existed around 500 CE.

00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:33.731
Over the next few hundred years,
Proto-Slavic split into three varieties:

00:01:33.802 --> 00:01:36.651
West Slavic, South Slavic and East Slavic.

00:01:36.676 --> 00:01:40.256
Which eventually diverged into branches
of the Slavic language family,

00:01:40.281 --> 00:01:42.040
containing numerous languages each.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.840
Russian is an East Slavic language

00:01:43.865 --> 00:01:46.790
along with Belarusian,
Ukrainian and also Rusyn.

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:49.023
At the time that these
three branches developed,

00:01:49.048 --> 00:01:52.682
there was a single Slavic literary
language that all Slavs could understand.

00:01:52.707 --> 00:01:54.762
That language was Old Church Slavonic.

00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:58.072
Russian is written in the Cyrillic script,

00:01:58.097 --> 00:02:01.115
which was created for the purpose
of writing Old Church Slavonic.

00:02:01.338 --> 00:02:02.639
Until the 14th century,

00:02:02.664 --> 00:02:06.072
the ancestors of modern day
Russians, Ukrainians and Belarusians,

00:02:06.097 --> 00:02:10.620
living in a state called Kievan Rus
spoke dialects of Old East Slavic.

00:02:10.645 --> 00:02:15.041
This spoken language existed alongside the
literary language of Old Church Slavonic,

00:02:15.066 --> 00:02:16.850
a situation of &lt;i&gt;diglossia&lt;/i&gt;.

00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:20.675
There is, however, some evidence
that people used to write

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.538
in the spoken vernacular for
some kinds of casual writing.

00:02:23.563 --> 00:02:26.935
Birch bark letters from the town
of Novogorod in the 14th century

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.450
are written in the vernacular language
and the concern topics of daily life.

00:02:30.475 --> 00:02:31.805
Old Church Slavonic, however,

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.023
was the literary and religious language.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.436
In the 13th century, Kievan Rus
was conquered by the Mongol Empire

00:02:38.461 --> 00:02:42.403
which brought a lot of foreign Turkic
loanwords into the East Slavic dialects,

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:45.411
especially words related to
political and financial matters.

00:02:45.446 --> 00:02:48.173
After the end of Mongol
rule in the 14th century,

00:02:48.198 --> 00:02:51.401
the Old East Slavic area was
split into two separate states,

00:02:51.536 --> 00:02:53.520
the Grand Duchy of Moscow to the east,

00:02:53.545 --> 00:02:55.687
and the Grand Duchy of Lithuania
to the west

00:02:55.712 --> 00:02:58.931
in the general area of
Ukraine, Belarus, and Lithuania.

00:02:58.956 --> 00:03:01.110
the oldest Slavic dialects
in those two states

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.719
began to split and develop
in different directions.

00:03:03.744 --> 00:03:07.194
In the west, the Ruthenian language
emerged with heavy Polish influence.

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:10.875
Ruthenian dialects would later develop
into Ukrainian and Belarusian.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.898
In the east, Russian emerged with much
more influence from Old Church Slavonic.

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:18.311
Modern Russian today resulted
from a kind of tug-of-war

00:03:18.336 --> 00:03:20.651
between Old Church Slavonic,
on the one hand,

00:03:20.676 --> 00:03:23.303
and the vernacular spoken dialects,
on the other hand.

00:03:23.399 --> 00:03:27.815
For example, in 1453 CE after
the fall of the Byzantine Empire,

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.286
Byzantine and Bulgarian scholars
began moving to Moscow,

00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:35.334
and they were upset that Old Church
Slavonic had become corrupted or Russified,

00:03:35.621 --> 00:03:39.094
and they tried to standardize the
language to remove these "impurities".

00:03:39.119 --> 00:03:41.970
So, a unique Russian language
had begun to emerge,

00:03:41.995 --> 00:03:45.383
but scholars pulled the language
back towards Old Church Slavonic.

00:03:45.846 --> 00:03:50.126
But, then during the rule of
Peter the Great from 1696 to 1725,

00:03:50.151 --> 00:03:52.556
the written language was
pulled in a different direction

00:03:52.581 --> 00:03:54.057
away from Old Church Slavonic.

00:03:54.082 --> 00:03:55.423
In order to increase literacy,

00:03:55.448 --> 00:03:58.120
he simplified the orthography
of the written language,

00:03:58.145 --> 00:04:00.546
eliminating some letters
and some diacritics.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:04.056
He also made great efforts to
modernize and westernize Russia,

00:04:04.081 --> 00:04:06.222
and started to orient
Russia towards Europe,

00:04:06.247 --> 00:04:09.651
and French became the common language
of the upper classes in Russia.

00:04:09.676 --> 00:04:10.746
As you might guess,

00:04:10.771 --> 00:04:14.020
this resulted in the adoption of
some French loanwords into Russian.

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:18.063
He also oversaw the intentional adoption
of specialized modern vocabulary

00:04:18.088 --> 00:04:21.285
from various European languages
 like French, Dutch and German.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.475
Peter the Great's reforms
were some of the first steps

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:26.038
in the development of
modern literary Russian,

00:04:26.063 --> 00:04:28.043
the basis of standard Russian today.

00:04:28.068 --> 00:04:31.672
Another influential person in the
development of modern literary Russian

00:04:31.697 --> 00:04:32.830
was Mikhail Lomonosov

00:04:33.272 --> 00:04:36.549
In 1755, he wrote a grammar
of the Russian language,

00:04:36.574 --> 00:04:38.383
which revolutionized the written language

00:04:38.408 --> 00:04:41.244
by combining Old Church Slavonic
with the spoken language.

00:04:41.269 --> 00:04:42.276
Throughout the grammar,

00:04:42.301 --> 00:04:45.465
he refers to Old Church Slavonic
as the language of High style,

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.890
and he refers to the spoken language
as the language of Low style,

00:04:48.915 --> 00:04:52.125
and he advocated a blended Middle
style for most forms of writing.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:54.870
The High style was basically
reserved for religious texts

00:04:54.895 --> 00:04:58.319
and the Low style was reserved for
the most casual written material.

00:04:58.344 --> 00:05:01.625
In the following decades, authors and
poets developed a new middle style

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.265
that would form the core of
Modern Standard Russian.

00:05:04.321 --> 00:05:07.352
The most influential was
the poet Alexander Pushkin,

00:05:07.377 --> 00:05:10.852
who was sometimes referred to as
the father of the modern Russian language.

00:05:11.006 --> 00:05:14.475
He fully developed the idea of
three different language registers,

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.030
high, middle and low,

00:05:16.055 --> 00:05:18.991
making the spoken vernacular a
legitimate part of the language.

00:05:19.053 --> 00:05:22.277
He also greatly expanded the
vocabulary of literary Russian

00:05:22.302 --> 00:05:25.635
by directly translating phrases
and idioms from other languages.

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:28.156
He greatly influenced
modern literary Russian,

00:05:28.181 --> 00:05:30.022
and as a result Modern Standard Russian

00:05:30.047 --> 00:05:32.966
which formed during this time
based on the literary language.

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:38.775
In the 20th century, Russian underwent
some small changes mainly in orthography.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.548
But, by far the most important thing
to happen to the language

00:05:41.573 --> 00:05:43.912
was the creation of the Soviet empire.

00:05:43.937 --> 00:05:47.333
Universal schooling and the mass
media spread modern Standard Russian

00:05:47.358 --> 00:05:49.730
and reduced dialectal variation in Russian.

00:05:49.755 --> 00:05:52.750
It also spread the language
over a vast geographic area,

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.345
covering all the member states of the USSR.

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:58.322
Today, Russian is still widely
spoken in all of those areas,

00:05:58.347 --> 00:06:00.950
and is a common lingua franca
between different ethnic groups,

00:06:00.975 --> 00:06:03.100
and between the citizens
of different countries.

00:06:03.125 --> 00:06:04.513
So, what is Russian like?

00:06:04.657 --> 00:06:05.657
Orthography.

00:06:05.682 --> 00:06:07.688
Russian is written in the Cyrillic script,

00:06:07.713 --> 00:06:12.000
an alphabet consisting of 33 letters which
were mostly developed from Greek letters.

00:06:12.025 --> 00:06:14.064
Some are the same as letters
of the Latin alphabet,

00:06:14.089 --> 00:06:16.254
but some of the sounds they
represent are different,

00:06:16.279 --> 00:06:17.279
like this one.

00:06:18.097 --> 00:06:19.097
Phonology.

00:06:19.396 --> 00:06:21.403
There are 21 consonants in Russian,

00:06:21.428 --> 00:06:25.047
but 15 of them can be pronounced in two
different ways either hard or soft,

00:06:25.072 --> 00:06:27.800
and you have to judge which sound
 to make by looking at the letter

00:06:27.825 --> 00:06:29.415
that comes after the consonant.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:31.127
The soft sound is palletized,

00:06:31.152 --> 00:06:33.771
meaning that part of your tongue
reaches the roof of your mouth.

00:06:33.819 --> 00:06:37.017
This makes the consonant kind of sound
like it's followed by a Y sound.

00:06:37.152 --> 00:06:38.183
Here are some examples.

00:06:38.358 --> 00:06:39.493
bak - byak

00:06:40.025 --> 00:06:41.532
bek - byek

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:43.500
mal - myal

00:06:43.628 --> 00:06:45.199
per - pyer

00:06:45.224 --> 00:06:47.001
sak - syak

00:06:47.676 --> 00:06:48.690
Phonology.

00:06:48.715 --> 00:06:50.944
There are six basic vowels in Russian,

00:06:50.969 --> 00:06:52.818
but there's a lot of
variation in the vowels

00:06:52.843 --> 00:06:56.405
depending on the surrounding sounds and
based on the stress pattern of the word.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:59.786
Vowels are often reduced when they
appear in an unstressed syllable,

00:06:59.811 --> 00:07:03.357
this can make it a challenge to pronounce
words correctly based on their spelling

00:07:03.382 --> 00:07:04.391
Grammar.

00:07:04.416 --> 00:07:07.945
Russian is a fusional language,
meaning that suffixes and prefixes

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:10.922
are added to root words to
communicate grammatical meaning.

00:07:10.947 --> 00:07:12.394
Let's look at nouns, first.

00:07:12.517 --> 00:07:14.533
There are six noun cases in Russian

00:07:14.558 --> 00:07:17.202
meaning that both the singular
and the plural form of a noun

00:07:17.227 --> 00:07:21.254
can take six different forms depending on
the grammatical function in the sentence.

00:07:21.467 --> 00:07:24.109
Let's look at an example
using the word for sister.

00:07:24.221 --> 00:07:25.959
Nominative case: sestra.

00:07:26.119 --> 00:07:28.745
Sestra chitayet -
"The sister reads".

00:07:29.228 --> 00:07:30.966
Genitive case: sestry.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:34.102
YA znayu imya sestry -
"I know the sister's name".

00:07:34.452 --> 00:07:36.428
Dative case: sestrye.

00:07:36.453 --> 00:07:40.238
Brat dal sestrye bilyet -
"The brother gave the sister a ticket".

00:07:40.675 --> 00:07:42.548
Accusative case: sestru.

00:07:42.573 --> 00:07:45.620
Brat vidit sestru -
"The brother sees the sister".

00:07:45.994 --> 00:07:48.073
Instrumental case: sestroy.

00:07:48.106 --> 00:07:50.732
YA zhivu s sestroy -
"I live with the sister".

00:07:51.145 --> 00:07:53.129
Prepositional case: sestrye.

00:07:53.352 --> 00:07:55.613
Na sestrye -
"On the sister".

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:59.450
On the side note, it's interesting
to notice the cognate vocabulary

00:07:59.475 --> 00:08:02.480
between Russian and English and
some other Indo-European languages.

00:08:02.553 --> 00:08:04.171
"sestra" is sister

00:08:04.227 --> 00:08:05.568
"brat" is brother

00:08:05.648 --> 00:08:07.266
"bilyet" is ticket

00:08:07.525 --> 00:08:09.477
and even the word for 'see' "vidit"

00:08:09.502 --> 00:08:12.850
reminds me of the Italian word
"vedere" which means 'to see'.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.725
So, here we see some clear indications of
Russian being an Indo-European language.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:18.403
Now, back to the noun cases.

00:08:18.428 --> 00:08:21.432
The case endings are different 
depending on the gender of the noun.

00:08:21.457 --> 00:08:23.552
Russian nouns are grouped
according to gender

00:08:23.577 --> 00:08:25.729
with masculine, feminine,
and neuter nouns.

00:08:25.754 --> 00:08:28.461
You can normally tell the nouns
gender based on its ending.

00:08:28.486 --> 00:08:30.860
Feminine nouns can take
either of these endings.

00:08:30.885 --> 00:08:33.440
Neuter nouns can take
either of these endings.

00:08:33.465 --> 00:08:36.471
And masculine nouns are the nouns
ending in any other letter.

00:08:36.496 --> 00:08:39.941
The one tricky exception is that
some nouns end in this letter,

00:08:39.966 --> 00:08:42.306
and these can be either
masculine or feminine,

00:08:42.331 --> 00:08:44.378
so you need to memorize
the gender of those ones.

00:08:44.424 --> 00:08:45.424
Adjectives.

00:08:46.029 --> 00:08:48.036
Adjectives and Russian
are quite complicated.

00:08:48.061 --> 00:08:52.545
They are inflected to agree with the noun
they modify, in gender, number and in case,

00:08:52.665 --> 00:08:56.782
That means that any particular adjectives
can have 24 different forms.

00:08:56.807 --> 00:08:58.300
Like this.
Wow.

00:08:59.173 --> 00:09:00.798
Let's look at a couple of examples,

00:09:00.823 --> 00:09:02.889
This is the adjective meaning "new".

00:09:03.172 --> 00:09:07.616
In the nominative case:
novvy - Eto novyy avtomobil'

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.140
This is a new car.

00:09:09.497 --> 00:09:13.941
In the genitive case:
novogo - tsvet novogo avtomobilya

00:09:14.078 --> 00:09:15.418
The new car's color.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:18.108
So, we can see how the ending
of the adjectives changes

00:09:18.133 --> 00:09:19.629
to make it agree with the noun.

00:09:19.803 --> 00:09:20.842
But, it's worth noting,

00:09:20.867 --> 00:09:23.418
that the inflectional endings
added to the adjectives

00:09:23.443 --> 00:09:25.365
are different from those added to the noun,

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:29.112
So, you need to memorize a big set of
endings for nouns and when to use them,

00:09:29.137 --> 00:09:32.195
but you have to memorize a whole
different set of endings for adjectives

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:33.328
and when to use those.

00:09:33.353 --> 00:09:34.374
Verbs.

00:09:34.399 --> 00:09:38.265
The Russian language has three
verb tenses: past, present and future.

00:09:38.392 --> 00:09:39.607
That sounds straightforward,

00:09:39.632 --> 00:09:43.315
but Russian verbs are also distinguished
based on something called aspect.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:47.505
There's the perfective aspect which means
that an action has been completed once

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:49.357
Then, there's the imperfective aspect

00:09:49.382 --> 00:09:52.595
which shows that the action is
still in progress, still ongoing,

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:56.157
or that it's an action that will be
recurring, will be repeated many times.

00:09:56.182 --> 00:09:58.751
In Russian verb,
there are two different verb stems

00:09:58.776 --> 00:10:02.724
for the imperfective and the perfective
aspect, basically like two different words.

00:10:02.749 --> 00:10:05.525
Let's look at an example for
the verb meaning to write.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:07.692
First the perfective: napisa.

00:10:07.779 --> 00:10:10.049
And the imperfective: pisa.

00:10:10.426 --> 00:10:14.390
Either of these stems can be conjugated
for past, present and future tenses.

00:10:14.583 --> 00:10:18.113
Let's look at an example of how aspect
changes the meaning of a sentence.

00:10:18.138 --> 00:10:20.899
This sentence uses the verb
with the perfective verb stem

00:10:20.924 --> 00:10:22.285
and is in the future tense

00:10:22.813 --> 00:10:24.312
YA napishu knigu

00:10:24.418 --> 00:10:26.374
This sentence means
"I will write a book",

00:10:26.399 --> 00:10:28.999
but when we take the aspect
into account the meaning is

00:10:29.024 --> 00:10:32.619
"I will write one specific book",
an action that will be completed.

00:10:32.668 --> 00:10:36.573
This next sentence uses the imperfective
verb stem, and is also in the future tense

00:10:36.708 --> 00:10:38.175
YA budu pisat' knigu

00:10:38.199 --> 00:10:41.262
The meaning of this one is more like
"I will be writing a book",

00:10:41.358 --> 00:10:44.145
in other words "I will be in
the process of writing a book",

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:45.585
at a certain time in the future.

00:10:45.625 --> 00:10:48.412
Or, if we use the plural form of book
then the meaning changes,

00:10:48.437 --> 00:10:50.206
it means "I will write books"

00:10:50.249 --> 00:10:52.048
YA budu pisat' knigi

00:10:52.592 --> 00:10:54.663
and that means
"I will write numerous books",

00:10:54.688 --> 00:10:55.722
a recurring action.

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:59.548
So, in that case the imperfective aspect
indicates a slightly different meaning

00:11:00.104 --> 00:11:01.214
Basic word order.

00:11:01.342 --> 00:11:03.731
The most common word order
in Russian is SVO,

00:11:03.756 --> 00:11:05.191
but because of the case system

00:11:05.216 --> 00:11:07.987
the word order is much more
flexible than it is in English.

00:11:08.012 --> 00:11:10.265
In English, we normally
judge the function of a word,

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:12.209
depending on where it is in the sentence.

00:11:12.234 --> 00:11:14.415
But, in Russian, we have the case system.

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:16.665
The case endings can tell us
the function of the word.

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:18.024
So, you can move the words around

00:11:18.049 --> 00:11:21.647
and we still understand what the subject
is, what the object is, and so on.

00:11:21.672 --> 00:11:25.098
So, a basic sentence might be:
Koshka poymala mysh',

00:11:25.218 --> 00:11:27.106
meaning the cat caught the mouse.

00:11:27.313 --> 00:11:30.179
But, you can also have:
Mysh' poymala koshka,

00:11:30.482 --> 00:11:32.704
or: poymala koshka mysh'

00:11:32.982 --> 00:11:34.601
and some other possible word order.

00:11:34.787 --> 00:11:37.204
So, the basic word order
is flexible like that

00:11:37.253 --> 00:11:39.817
However, in some cases
the context or the style

00:11:39.842 --> 00:11:42.554
that you are trying to communicate
 in will determine the word order

00:11:42.569 --> 00:11:43.569
Articles.

00:11:44.023 --> 00:11:46.923
Like most Slavic languages, Russian
doesn't have any articles,

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:49.059
it doesn't have words like 'a' or 'the'.

00:11:49.249 --> 00:11:50.929
If you have any Russian speaking riends,

00:11:50.954 --> 00:11:53.040
who drop the articles when
they're speaking English,

00:11:53.065 --> 00:11:54.238
that's probably the reason.

00:11:54.263 --> 00:11:55.948
So, how hard is Russian to learn?

00:11:56.011 --> 00:11:58.557
Well, according to the
American Foreign Service Institute,

00:11:58.582 --> 00:12:00.423
Russian is a category IV language.

00:12:00.552 --> 00:12:02.864
The most difficult
category is category V,

00:12:02.888 --> 00:12:05.532
and that includes Japanese,
Mandarin, and Arabic.

00:12:05.557 --> 00:12:07.054
But, Russian is in category IV,

00:12:07.079 --> 00:12:10.326
that means that it's relatively
difficult for English speakers,

00:12:10.351 --> 00:12:11.874
but not the most difficult.

00:12:11.899 --> 00:12:15.774
The most challenging things will likely
be the inflection of nouns and adjectives,

00:12:15.799 --> 00:12:17.989
as well as the system of verbal aspect.

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:20.822
Some people mistakenly assume
that the Cyrillic alphabet

00:12:20.847 --> 00:12:23.310
is going to be the most challenging
part of learning Russian,

00:12:23.335 --> 00:12:26.276
but people actually learn that
fairly quickly once they start.

00:12:26.301 --> 00:12:29.562
Russian is a major global language
that will be very useful to you

00:12:29.587 --> 00:12:33.004
if you spend any time in Russia or 
in any of the former Soviet republics,

00:12:33.029 --> 00:12:34.838
so your efforts will be well rewarded.

00:12:35.064 --> 00:12:36.444
So, the question of the day.

00:12:36.469 --> 00:12:38.060
For people who have studied Russian:

00:12:38.084 --> 00:12:39.864
What did you find challenging about Russian

00:12:39.889 --> 00:12:42.015
and what did you find
straightforward about Russian?

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:43.642
And, for native speakers of Russian:

00:12:43.667 --> 00:12:46.397
Russia is a huge country,
do you notice a lot of variation

00:12:46.422 --> 00:12:49.350
in the way that Russian is spoken in
different parts of the countries?

00:12:49.375 --> 00:12:51.834
Are there noticeable accents
or dialects these days?

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:54.040
Let us know in the comments down below.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:58.146
